course: dictionary
lesson: 8643
title: The seagull and its many cousins
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A seagull is a large grey and white bird with a loud cry. It lives near the sea, but you will often find it by rivers and in towns. Context helps you learn more about a word, so look at the sentences around it.
explain: «There is no single bird called a seagull: there are many kinds of gull, like the herring gull and the black-headed gull.» The clue is in the word kinds. Seagull is a common name for several kinds of gull, not one special bird. Many gulls live far inland, so you may see one a long way from the beach.
explain: «Feeding seagulls teaches them to snatch food, so you eat in a sheltered spot and do not feed them.» The word teaches is the clue: gulls learn to grab food when people feed them. A sheltered spot is a safe place, out of the wind, where your food is less likely to be stolen.
fact: A seagull is a large grey and white bird with a loud cry.
fact: «Seagull» is a common name for many kinds of gull, such as the herring gull and the black-headed gull.
fact: Many gulls live inland, around rubbish tips, fields and city roofs, not only by the sea.
fact: Seagulls snatch chips and sandwiches at the beach because people have taught them to take food.
fact: Do not feed seagulls, and keep your food covered at the seaside.
exercise: What is a seagull? | A sort of sailing boat | A small brown bird that lives in trees | *A large grey and white bird with a loud cry | A fish that swims in rivers
exercise: Ben thinks a seagull is one special bird. What is the truth? | Seagulls are only found on the beach | *There are many kinds of gull, like the herring gull and the black-headed gull | One single bird with one name | A seagull is a type of fish
exercise: «Feeding seagulls teaches them to snatch food, so you eat in a sheltered spot.» What does sheltered mean? | Out in the open, where the wind blows freely | *Safe and out of the wind | Very bright and sunny | Full of water after the rain
test: «Many of them live far inland, around rubbish tips, fields and city roofs.» Where do many gulls live? | *Far inland, around fields and city roofs | Only on cliffs by the sea | In the middle of the ocean | Under the ground in holes
test: What does feeding seagulls teach them to do? | Sing louder | *Snatch food | Sleep all day | Fly backwards
exam: What is the best advice at the seaside about seagulls? | Feed them from your hand | Give them your sandwich | Chase them along the beach | *Do not feed them, and keep your food covered
